<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>A credential value originating from <code>KyotoUAuth</code> reaches a
logging call. Anything written to <code>logging</code>, <code>print</code>,
<code>warnings.warn</code>, <code>sys.stdout.write</code> /
<code>sys.stderr.write</code>, or <code>traceback.print_*</code> is
collected by the host's stdout/stderr and forwarded to whatever
observability pipeline the user has wired up. A password or TOTP secret
that lands there is, for practical purposes, leaked.</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>Never log a credential. If a debug message needs to reference an OTP,
log a fixed placeholder (<code>"otp=&lt;redacted&gt;"</code>) or a length
indicator only. Stack traces that would otherwise dump locals should be
suppressed via <code>--tb=short</code> in pytest or
<code>logger.exception(..., exc_info=False)</code> in production
handlers.</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Bad — the OTP value is rendered into the log line:</p>
<sample language="python">
otp = self._auth._resolve_otp()
logger.debug("submitting otp %s", otp)  # leaks
</sample>

<p>Good — log only metadata:</p>
<sample language="python">
otp = self._auth._resolve_otp()
logger.debug("submitting otp (len=%d)", len(otp))
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>CWE-532: Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File
(<a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html">cwe.mitre.org/532</a>).</li>
<li>OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet — "Data to exclude" section
(<a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html">owasp.org</a>).</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
